IMPORT prelude

-- Trusts (Hague Convention) Act 1991 (Cth) and the Convention in its
-- Schedule — THE NOUNS, THE RULES AND THE TOP-LEVEL GOAL. Source:
-- ../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04125.txt (compilation No. 1, 1 July
-- 2016). The settlor, trustee or beneficiary asks: "Does the Convention apply
-- to my trust; which law governs it; must it be recognised as a trust?"

§ `Nouns`

-- Articles 6-7: which law governs.
DECLARE `Governing law` IS ONE OF
    `the law the settlor chose`
    `the law with which the trust is most closely connected`

DECLARE `Trust case` HAS
    -- Article 2
    `assets are under a trustee's control for a beneficiary or a specified purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the assets are a separate fund, not part of the trustee's own estate`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `title stands in the trustee's name or another's on the trustee's behalf`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the trustee must manage the assets under the terms, and is accountable`   IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 3
    `the trust was created voluntarily`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is evidenced in writing`                                              IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 7(1)
    `the conflict is solely between laws of Australian States or Territories` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 6
    `the settlor chose a law, expressly or by implication`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the chosen law provides for trusts of this category`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Articles 5, 7
    `the most closely connected law provides for trusts of this category`     IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 11
    `the trust was created in accordance with the governing law`              IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 13
    `its significant elements are more closely connected with States without trusts` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Article 18
    `applying the Convention would be manifestly incompatible with public policy` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Trust position` HAS
    `the Convention applies to my trust`                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `my trust is governed by`                                                 IS A MAYBE `Governing law`
    `my trust must be recognised`                                             IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN

§ `Rules`

-- Article 2: the trust and its characteristics.
@ref Article 2
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a trust for the Convention` c IF
        c's `assets are under a trustee's control for a beneficiary or a specified purpose`
    AND c's `the assets are a separate fund, not part of the trustee's own estate`
    AND c's `title stands in the trustee's name or another's on the trustee's behalf`
    AND c's `the trustee must manage the assets under the terms, and is accountable`

-- Article 6: the settlor's choice, unless the chosen law does not provide for
-- trusts of this category; then Article 7.
@ref Articles 6-7
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`
GIVETH A `Governing law`
`the governing law for` c MEANS
    IF c's `the settlor chose a law, expressly or by implication` AND c's `the chosen law provides for trusts of this category`
    THEN `the law the settlor chose`
    ELSE `the law with which the trust is most closely connected`

-- Article 5: not to the extent that the law specified by Chapter II does not
-- provide for trusts or the category.
@ref Article 5
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the governing law provides for it` c IF
    CONSIDER `the governing law for` c
    WHEN `the law the settlor chose` THEN c's `the chosen law provides for trusts of this category`
    WHEN `the law with which the trust is most closely connected` THEN c's `the most closely connected law provides for trusts of this category`

-- s 6, s 7(1); Articles 2, 3, 5.
@ref s 7(1), Articles 2-3, 5
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Convention applies` c IF
        NOT (c's `the conflict is solely between laws of Australian States or Territories`)
    AND `it is a trust for the Convention` c
    AND c's `the trust was created voluntarily`
    AND c's `it is evidenced in writing`
    AND `the governing law provides for it` c

-- Article 11: a trust created under the governing law "shall be recognized".
-- Article 13: no State is bound to recognise one whose significant elements
-- lie with non-trust States: NOTHING. Article 18: may be disregarded on public
-- policy: NOTHING too (changed after the independent pass).
@ref Articles 11, 13, 18
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`must my trust be recognised` c MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the Convention applies` c) THEN NOTHING
        IF c's `applying the Convention would be manifestly incompatible with public policy` THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (c's `the trust was created in accordance with the governing law`) THEN JUST FALSE
        IF c's `its significant elements are more closely connected with States without trusts` THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 6, s 7, Articles 2-7, 11, 13, 18
@export default The position of a settlor, trustee or beneficiary of a trust under the Trusts (Hague Convention) Act 1991 (Cth), which gives the Hague Trusts Convention the force of law: whether the Convention applies (a trust with the Article 2 characteristics, created voluntarily and evidenced in writing, under a law that provides for it, and not a conflict solely between Australian States or Territories: s 7, Articles 2-5); which law governs it (the law the settlor chose, if it provides for trusts of the kind, otherwise the most closely connected law: Articles 6-7); and whether it must be recognised as a trust (Article 11; no obligation where its significant elements lie with non-trust States, Article 13; nor where manifestly against public policy, Article 18). DRAFT, not reviewed by an Australian lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Trust case`  @desc The trust's characteristics, creation and writing, any choice of law and whether the laws provide for trusts, and the recognition facts
GIVETH A `Trust position`
`the trust position for` c MEANS
    `Trust position` OF `the Convention applies` c,
                        (IF `the Convention applies` c THEN JUST (`the governing law for` c) ELSE NOTHING),
                        `must my trust be recognised` c
